\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
An ordinary Edgeworth box in the exam's notation.
\series default
 The two goods are consumption in dry weather, 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i1}$
\end_inset

, and consumption in rainy weather, 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i2}$
\end_inset

. Final holdings and security trades satisfy 
\begin_inset Formula \[
 c_{i1}=\omega_{i1}+a_{i1},\qquad
 c_{i2}=\omega_{i2}+a_{i2},\qquad i\in\{A,B\}.
\]
\end_inset

Farm A is irrigated with endowment 
\begin_inset Formula $\omega_A=(2,2)$
\end_inset

; Farm B relies on rainfall with endowment 
\begin_inset Formula $\omega_B=(1,4)$
\end_inset

. Total resources are 
\begin_inset Formula $(3,6)$
\end_inset

, so the box has width 3 and height 6. The given Bernoulli utility is 
\begin_inset Formula $u(c)=-1/c$
\end_inset

. Use the supplied equally weighted expected utility as the ordinary two good preference index: 
\begin_inset Formula \[
 U_i(c_{i1},c_{i2})=-\frac1{2c_{i1}}-\frac1{2c_{i2}},\qquad c_{i1},c_{i2}>0.
\]
\end_inset

The goods remain state contingent: each is grain delivered in a different weather state.
\end_layout

