\begin_layout Standard
The consumption budget is 
\begin_inset Formula \[
 q_1c_{i1}+q_2c_{i2}\le q_1\omega_{i1}+q_2\omega_{i2}.
\]
\end_inset

Substituting 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{is}=c_{is}-\omega_{is}$
\end_inset

 gives the equivalent security budget 
\begin_inset Formula \[
 q_1a_{i1}+q_2a_{i2}\le0.
\]
\end_inset

Divide by 
\begin_inset Formula $q_2>0$
\end_inset

 and express wealth in units of rainy state consumption: 
\begin_inset Formula \[
 p:=\frac{q_1}{q_2},\qquad m_i:=p\omega_{i1}+\omega_{i2},\qquad
 pc_{i1}+c_{i2}\le m_i.
\]
\end_inset

Thus 
\begin_inset Formula $m_A=2p+2$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $m_B=p+4$
\end_inset

. Monotonicity makes each budget bind at an optimum. Feasibility and security clearing are 
\begin_inset Formula \[
 c_{A1}+c_{B1}=3,\qquad c_{A2}+c_{B2}=6,\qquad
 a_{As}+a_{Bs}=0\quad(s=1,2).
\]
\end_inset

The two contingent commodities are distinct because one claim delivers only in dry weather and the other only in rainy weather.
\end_layout

