\begin_layout Standard
For either household the marginal rate of substitution is 
\begin_inset Formula \[
 \MRS_i=\frac{\partial_1 U_i}{\partial_2 U_i}
 =\left(\frac{c_{i2}}{c_{i1}}\right)^2.
\]
\end_inset

All consumption is strictly positive. An efficient allocation must therefore have equal MRS. B's consumption is measured from the opposite origin, with 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{B1}=3-c_{A1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{B2}=6-c_{A2}$
\end_inset

. Consequently, 
\begin_inset Formula \[
 \left(\frac{c_{A2}}{c_{A1}}\right)^2
 =\left(\frac{6-c_{A2}}{3-c_{A1}}\right)^2
 \quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad c_{A2}=2c_{A1}.
\]
\end_inset

Hence the full candidate contract curve is 
\begin_inset Formula \[
 \boxed{c_A=(t,2t),\qquad c_B=(3-t,6-2t),\qquad 0<t<3.}
\]
\end_inset

It is the open diagonal joining the two origins of the rectangular box.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Global sufficiency.
\series default
 At every allocation on this line, both MRS equal 4, so the same positive price vector 
\begin_inset Formula $(4,1)$
\end_inset

 supports both indifference curves. Let 
\begin_inset Formula $c_i^*$
\end_inset

 be a candidate and 
\begin_inset Formula $\widetilde c_i$
\end_inset

 any other positive bundle. Concavity gives 
\begin_inset Formula \[
 U_i(\widetilde c_i)-U_i(c_i^*)
 \le \partial_2U_i(c_i^*)\bigl[4(\widetilde c_{i1}-c_{i1}^*)
 +(\widetilde c_{i2}-c_{i2}^*)\bigr].
\]
\end_inset

Since 
\begin_inset Formula $\partial_2U_i(c_i^*)>0$
\end_inset

, a weak improvement requires a nonnegative expenditure change at 
\begin_inset Formula $(4,1)$
\end_inset

, and a strict improvement requires a positive change. Summing the two expenditure changes gives zero by resource feasibility, ruling out a Pareto improvement. Every displayed allocation is therefore globally efficient. This proves sufficiency as well as necessity. The limiting points 
\begin_inset Formula $t=0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $t=3$
\end_inset

 give one household zero consumption and are excluded by the strict domain.
\end_layout

