\series bold
Ordinary consumer demand.
\series default
 Fix any relative price 
\begin_inset Formula $p>0$
\end_inset

 and wealth 
\begin_inset Formula $m_i>0$
\end_inset

. Tangency and the binding budget give 
\begin_inset Formula \[
 \left(\frac{c_{i2}}{c_{i1}}\right)^2=p,\qquad
 pc_{i1}+c_{i2}=m_i.
\]
\end_inset

Thus 
\begin_inset Formula \[
 \boxed{c_{i1}(p)=\frac{m_i}{p+\sqrt p},\qquad
 c_{i2}(p)=\frac{\sqrt p\,m_i}{p+\sqrt p},\qquad
 m_i=p\omega_{i1}+\omega_{i2}.}
\]
\end_inset

This demand is globally optimal. Substitute 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i2}=m_i-pc_{i1}$
\end_inset

 into utility. On 
\begin_inset Formula $0<c_{i1}<m_i/p$
\end_inset

, the resulting one variable objective satisfies 
\begin_inset Formula \[
 f_i'(c_{i1})=\frac1{2c_{i1}^2}-\frac{p}{2(m_i-pc_{i1})^2},\qquad
 f_i''(c_{i1})=-\frac1{c_{i1}^3}-\frac{p^2}{(m_i-pc_{i1})^3}<0.
\]
\end_inset

The displayed positive affordable candidate is stationary, and strict concavity makes it a global maximum. No multiplier calculation is needed.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Clear the Edgeworth box.
\series default
 Both demands satisfy 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i2}=\sqrt p\,c_{i1}$
\end_inset

. Summing and imposing resource feasibility gives 
\begin_inset Formula \[
 6=3\sqrt p\quad\Longrightarrow\quad p=4.
\]
\end_inset

At prices 
\begin_inset Formula $(p,1)=(4,1)$
\end_inset

, endowment wealths are 
\begin_inset Formula $m_A=10$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $m_B=8$
\end_inset

. Substitution gives 
\begin_inset Formula \[
 \boxed{c_A=\left(\frac53,\frac{10}3\right),\qquad
 c_B=\left(\frac43,\frac83\right).}
\]
\end_inset

Geometrically, A's equilibrium is the intersection of its budget 
\begin_inset Formula $4c_{A1}+c_{A2}=10$
\end_inset

, through the endowment 
\begin_inset Formula $(2,2)$
\end_inset

, and the contract curve 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{A2}=2c_{A1}$
\end_inset

. B's budget is 
\begin_inset Formula $4c_{B1}+c_{B2}=8$
\end_inset

. Both demands are optimal and sum to 
\begin_inset Formula $(3,6)$
\end_inset

, establishing equilibrium existence directly.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
State prices.
\series default
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $p=q_1/q_2=4$
\end_inset

, normalized prices are 
\begin_inset Formula $q=(4/5,1/5)$
\end_inset

 and the full positive price set is 
\begin_inset Formula \[
 \boxed{q=k(4,1),\qquad k>0.}
\]
\end_inset

Consumption is unchanged by price scaling. Normalized wealths are 
\begin_inset Formula $q\cdot\omega_A=2$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $q\cdot\omega_B=8/5$
\end_inset

; these use a different accounting unit from 
\begin_inset Formula $m_A=10,m_B=8$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

