\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Contract curve.
\series default
 For either consumer, 
\begin_inset Formula $\MRS_i=y_i/x_i$
\end_inset

. Interior efficiency requires equal MRS, so 
\begin_inset Formula \[
 \frac{y_A}{x_A}=\frac{4-y_A}{4-x_A}
 \quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad y_A=x_A.
\]
\end_inset

Thus the candidate Pareto set is the open diagonal 
\begin_inset Formula \[
 \boxed{A=(t,t),\qquad B=(4-t,4-t),\qquad 0<t<4.}
\]
\end_inset

The endpoints are excluded by strictly positive consumption.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Global efficiency by one inequality.
\series default
 Arithmetic geometric means give 
\begin_inset Formula \[
 U_A+U_B=\sqrt{x_Ay_A}+\sqrt{x_By_B}
 \le\frac{x_A+y_A+x_B+y_B}{2}=4.
\]
\end_inset

Every displayed diagonal allocation attains 4, since 
\begin_inset Formula $U_A=t$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $U_B=4-t$
\end_inset

. A Pareto improvement would increase this sum above 4, which is impossible. This proves sufficiency; the MRS argument proves necessity.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Core.
\series default
 Both consumers initially obtain 
\begin_inset Formula $\sqrt3$
\end_inset

. Individual rationality on the contract curve requires 
\begin_inset Formula \[
 t\ge\sqrt3,\qquad 4-t\ge\sqrt3.
\]
\end_inset

Using the supplied core criterion, the frictionless core is therefore 
\begin_inset Formula \[
 \boxed{\sqrt3\le t\le4-\sqrt3,\qquad A=(t,t),\quad B=(4-t,4-t).}
\]
\end_inset

It is a closed segment of the open contract curve. Its endpoints are included because individual rationality is weak.
\end_layout

