\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $B=(2-d,2+d)$
\end_inset

, both consumers have utility 
\begin_inset Formula $\sqrt{4-d^2}$
\end_inset

. The preliminary offer exchanges 
\begin_inset Formula $d/2$
\end_inset

 units of A's 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $d/2$
\end_inset

 units of B's 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

, leaving 
\begin_inset Formula \[
 A'=\left(2+\frac d2,2-\frac d2\right),\qquad
 B'=\left(2-\frac d2,2+\frac d2\right).
\]
\end_inset

Each new utility is 
\begin_inset Formula $\sqrt{4-d^2/4}$
\end_inset

. Hence each consumer's gross gain is 
\begin_inset Formula \[
 \boxed{\Delta(d)=\sqrt{4-d^2/4}-\sqrt{4-d^2}>0,\qquad 0<d\le1.}
\]
\end_inset

The inequality is strict because the radicand rises by 
\begin_inset Formula $3d^2/4>0$
\end_inset

. All bundles are positive and the transfers preserve total resources. Both consumers benefit before costs. Part (d) checks acceptance when attention costs are included. An initial MRS describes a local trade rate; a finite offer requires the direct utility comparison above.
\end_layout

